rpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use millimetre with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify centimetre and explain why it matters.","I can practise metre independently on a new prompt.","I can notice kilometre needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Length units relate by place-value style scales: 10 mm = 1 cm; 100 cm = 1 m; 1000 m = 1 km. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Length units relate by place-value style scales: 10 mm = 1 cm","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about millimetre with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA pencil might be 15 cm; a room 5 m; a town trip 3 km. Choosing the unit that fits the size makes measures readable.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich unit suits: width of a fingernail; length of a soccer field; distance between cities?\n\nFEEDBACK\nmm (or cm); m; km.","explain":"Length units relate by place-value style scales: 10 mm = 1 cm; 100 cm = 1 m; 1000 m = 1 km.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Measuring a playground in millimetres alone creates huge, hard-to-read numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Length unit relationships.","caption":"Choose unit that fits size.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">10 mm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1 cm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">1000 m</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1 km</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A pencil might be 15 cm; a room 5 m; a town trip 3 km. 